WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.960
ROBERT KUBIS: With almost
500 sessions at Next 2018,

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.790
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.150
So if you didn't see what's
new with Cloud Spanner,

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:10.000
then stay tuned, because
here's the Rewind.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.610
Cloud Spanner is Google's
answer to have it all.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:22.630
No compromises on
performance, horizontal scale,

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:25.970
strong consistency by having
familiar relational semantics,

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:27.510
schemas, and SQL.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.810
And the best, it comes as
a fully managed service

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:33.110
available in 13 Google
Cloud Platform regions.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:35.480
Over the last year,
many things got added.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.130
We have expanded to more
regions around the globe,

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.340
added admin and
data access logging,

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.560
more metrics and integrations
into Stackdriver.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.660
For instance, the open
census integration

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:47.030
for better
observability, and also

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:49.430
added multiple compliance
certifications.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:51.830
Another cool new feature
is commit timestamps,

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:53.780
which is heavily used
internally and was

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:55.760
asked for by our customers.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.140
Commit timestamps allow
you to transactionally log

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.840
when a transaction has committed
as part of your modified rows.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.560
But we didn't stop there.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.480
More features are
launching to improve

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:08.270
manageability and
development on Cloud Spanner.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:11.600
Supported by demos from
Deepti and colleagues,

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.930
they show the new push button
import/export functionality

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:17.960
for disaster recovery or
moving data between databases

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.850
for development and testing.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.850
Next, you get a sneak preview
into a long-awaited feature,

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.740
like DML, which
allows you to run

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.490
complex queries with conditional
updates directly from the UI.

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:31.490
DML will also enable
full-fledged JDBC driver

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.440
support coming soon.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.620
Another feature previewed
are query statistics,

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.470
which allows you to get insights
into your worst-performing

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:42.800
and most expensive SQL queries
for better application insights

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.570
and performance tuning.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:47.030
During the talk, Deepti
invites Nexus Health,

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.860
a customer on Cloud Spanner.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:51.590
Nexus Health has built their
own electronic medical record

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.570
system, called
Clinical Workstation,

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:56.170
on top of positional databases.

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:59.420
It became so popular that
over 30 hospitals in Belgium

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:00.800
started using it.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.590
Managing that complex system
with high availability

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.490
requirements has
become a burden.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:08.690
So to support the rapid growth
with the need for reliability

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:10.880
and availability
while concentrating

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.190
on building the application
rather than maintaining

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:15.080
the infrastructure,
Nexus Health has

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.990
started building the
next version of MOAPR,

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:21.710
Mother Of All Patient Records,
on top of Cloud Spanner.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:24.650
If this sparked your interest,
please watch the full talk.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.270
And if you're ready
to dive deeper

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:29.120
into improving your applications
built on Cloud Spanner,

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.970
check out my talk on optimizing
applications, schemas,

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:33.350
and query design.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:36.307
Links to both talks can be
found in the description below.

00:02:36.307 --> 00:02:38.390
And don't forget to subscribe
to the Cloud YouTube

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:41.240
channel for more great
Next Rewind content.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:42.620
Thank you for watching.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:46.270
[MUSIC PLAYING]

